  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 26-year-old African-American woman is highly suggestive of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). Key symptoms include pleuritic chest pain, arthralgia, malar rash, hematuria, and a history of miscarriages, all of which align with SLE. In SLE, it is common to find low serum levels of complement components C3 and C4 due to complement consumption in immune complex-mediated disease. The reasoning chains supporting low C3 and C4 levels are consistent with standard medical consensus for SLE. The retrieved evidence paths related to lupus nephritis and arthralgia support the diagnosis of SLE. The option of anti-histone antibodies is less likely as it is more specific to drug-induced lupus, which typically presents with milder symptoms and lacks the full spectrum of SLE manifestations. The evidence paths related to sarcoidosis and hilar lymphadenopathy are not relevant to this case, as the clinical presentation does not align with sarcoidosis. Therefore, the most likely finding upon further evaluation is low serum levels of C3 and C4, consistent with SLE.",
